2026-09-24T12:45:56.821166Z TRACE tungstenite::protocol: Received message {"type": "response.output_item.done", "item": {"type": "message", "content": [{"type": "output_text", "text": "A:\n\nRivers begin as small acts of gravity. Rain gathers in hollows, snow loosens from mountain slopes, and springs push cold water through stone. Each trickle joins another until a current finds its direction. From there, a river becomes both traveler and maker, carrying soil while cutting valleys, feeding wetlands, and opening paths across continents.\n\nPeople have always built beside rivers. Water supports crops, carries boats, powers mills, and cools crowded cities. The Nile shaped ancient Egyptian farming. The Mississippi linked farms and factories to distant ports. The Ganges holds religious meaning for millions, while the Amazon moves more water than any other river on Earth. Every river gathers human stories along with rain.\n\nA river also records what happens upstream. Fertilizer washed from fields can feed algae far away. Plastic dropped in a street may enter a drain, pass through tributaries, and reach the sea. Dams can provide electricity and flood control, yet they also block fish migrations and trap sediment that once renewed downstream land. Choices made at one bend rarely stay there.\n\nHealthy rivers need room. Floodplains absorb high water, forests shade channels, and fallen trees create shelter for insects and fish. Restoring these features can reduce flood damage while helping wildlife return. Some cities now remove concrete walls, reopen buried streams, and build parks where water can spread safely after storms.\n\nNo river remains still, even when its surface looks calm. Channels shift. Banks collapse and form again. Water evaporates, rises, falls, and eventually returns as rain or snow. A river is a moving connection between mountain and ocean, weather and soil, one community and the next. To care for a river is to recognize that life downstream depends on attention upstream. Its passage reminds us that places often share the same water and the same fate.\n\nA\n\nB spawn error:\n\n`collab spawn failed: agent thread limit reached`"}]}}
